      "math_academy_id": "q-39094",
      "sequence_position": 8,
      "problem": "Given that $(x+1)$ is a factor of $f(x)=-2{x}^{3}-4{x}^{2}-5x-3,$ which of the following curves is generated by $y=f(x)?$\n\n(*Note: Figures not drawn to scale*)",
      "worked_solution": "First, we need to factor the polynomial. We are told that $(x+1)$ is a factor of $f(x),$ so $(x+1)$ divides $f(x)$ with zero remainder. To begin factoring $f(x),$ we can divide it by $(x+1)$ using synthetic division:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {x}^{3} & {x}^{2} & {x}^{1} & {x}^{0} \\\\ -1 & -2 & -4 & -5 & -3 \\\\  &  & 2 & 2 & 3 \\\\  & -2 & -2 & -3 & 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{f(x)}{x+1} & =-2{x}^{2}-2x-3 \\\\  & =-(2{x}^{2}+2x+3),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\nf(x)=-(x+1)(2{x}^{2}+2x+3).\n$$\n\nThe equation is now fully factored. Note that the discriminant of $2{x}^{2}+2x+3=0$ is \n\n$$\nD=(2)^{2}-4(2)(3)=-20<0,\n$$\n\nwhich implies that the quadratic factor has no real roots. So by the zero-product rule, we deduce that $f(x)$ has a single root at $x=-1.$ \n\nWe also find the $y$-intercept of the curve by substituting the value $x=0$ into the equation:\n\n$$\ny=-2(0)^{3}-4(0)^{2}-5(0)-3=-3\n$$\n\nSo, the curve:\n\n- has a $y$-intercept at $(0,-3),$\n\n- passes through the $x$-axis at the single root $x=-1,$ and\n\n- has no more $x$-intercepts.\n\nBecause the leading coefficient is negative, the graph has an $\\text{S}$-shape. Therefore, the graph looks as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14240558/assets/d391911307bc35f7481fe8b3a0b3871d1c4b75cc44d4459018fe00b14133f4f9.png)",
